Lab 18: Automating ODF Deployment with Ansible
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Understand the fundamentals of automating OpenShift Data Foundation (ODF) deployment using Ansible
Create and execute Ansible playbooks to deploy and configure ODF components
Automate the creation of Persistent Volume Claims (PVCs) and Object Bucket Claims (OBCs)
Build a management playbook that checks ODF cluster health and reports on storage capacity
Apply Ansible templating patterns and best practices for infrastructure as code in storage management
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of OpenShift Container Platform concepts
Familiarity with Kubernetes storage concepts (PVs, PVCs, StorageClasses)
Basic knowledge of Ansible automation and YAML syntax
Understanding of OpenShift Data Foundation fundamentals
Experience with command-line interface operations
Access to an OpenShift cluster (4.12+) with cluster-admin privileges, or a practice/sandbox cluster where you are authorized to install operators and create storage resources
This lab assumes a pre-configured practice environment (a personal sandbox cluster or an Al Nafi-style ready-to-use cloud machine) where Ansible, the OpenShift CLI, and network access to the cluster are already available. If you are setting up your own workstation, install the required tools with:

python3 -m pip install --user ansible
ansible-galaxy collection install kubernetes.core
ansible-galaxy collection install community.general
You will also need the oc CLI installed and logged in to your cluster (oc login) before running any playbook in this lab.

Lab Environment Setup
Your lab environment should include:

OpenShift Container Platform cluster (4.12+)
Ansible (2.14+) with the kubernetes.core collection installed
Pre-installed OpenShift CLI (oc), already authenticated to the cluster
Cluster-admin permissions, or equivalent RBAC allowing namespace, operator, and storage resource creation
Verify your environment before starting:

oc whoami
oc get nodes
ansible --version
You should see your current user, a list of cluster nodes, and the installed Ansible version with no errors.

Task 1: Write Ansible Playbooks to Deploy and Configure ODF
Subtask 1.1: Set Up Ansible Environment for ODF
