Subtask 1.2: Create Inventory and Variables
Create a Local Inventory
Since all tasks in this lab run against the OpenShift API from the control node (not over SSH to remote hosts), we use a simple localhost inventory.

cat > inventory/hosts << 'EOF'
[local]
localhost ansible_connection=local ansible_python_interpreter=/usr/bin/python3
EOF
Create Variables File for ODF Configuration
cat > vars/odf-config.yml << 'EOF'
---
# ODF Configuration Variables
odf_namespace: openshift-storage
odf_subscription_name: odf-operator
odf_channel: stable-4.12
odf_source: redhat-operators

# Storage Configuration
storage_class_name: ocs-storagecluster-ceph-rbd
object_storage_class: ocs-storagecluster-ceph-rgw
storage_cluster_name: ocs-storagecluster

# Storage Capacity
storage_capacity: 2Ti
replica_count: 3
EOF
Deliverable: inventory/hosts and vars/odf-config.yml exist and contain valid YAML. Validate with:

ansible-inventory -i inventory/hosts --list
python3 -c "import yaml; yaml.safe_load(open('vars/odf-config.yml'))" && echo "YAML OK"
Expected result: the inventory command prints a JSON structure containing localhost, and YAML OK is printed with no parsing errors.

Subtask 1.3: Create the ODF Deployment Playbook
Create the main playbook that installs the ODF operator subscription and creates a StorageCluster. This mirrors the standard operator-based installation flow used in production ODF deployments.

cat > playbooks/deploy-odf.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Deploy OpenShift Data Foundation
  hosts: localhost
  gather_facts: false
  vars_files:
    - ../vars/odf-config.yml

  tasks:
    - name: Create ODF namespace
      kubernetes.core.k8s:
        name: "{{ odf_namespace }}"
        api_version: v1
        kind: Namespace
        state: present
        definition:
          metadata:
            labels:
              openshift.io/cluster-monitoring: "true"

    - name: Create OperatorGroup for ODF
      kubernetes.core.k8s:
        state: present
        definition:
          apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1
          kind: OperatorGroup
          metadata:
            name: openshift-storage-operatorgroup
            namespace: "{{ odf_namespace }}"
          spec:
            targetNamespaces:
              - "{{ odf_namespace }}"

    - name: Create ODF Subscription
      kubernetes.core.k8s:
        state: present
        definition:
          apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
          kind: Subscription
          metadata:
            name: "{{ odf_subscription_name }}"
            namespace: "{{ odf_namespace }}"
          spec:
            channel: "{{ odf_channel }}"
            name: odf-operator
            source: "{{ odf_source }}"
            sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
            installPlanApproval: Automatic

    - name: Wait for ODF operator deployment to exist and become available
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: apps/v1
        kind: Deployment
        name: ocs-operator
        namespace: "{{ odf_namespace }}"
      register: ocs_operator_deployment
      until: ocs_operator_deployment.resources | length > 0
      retries: 30
      delay: 20

    - name: Create StorageCluster
      kubernetes.core.k8s:
        state: present
        definition:
          apiVersion: ocs.openshift.io/v1
          kind: StorageCluster
          metadata:
            name: "{{ storage_cluster_name }}"
            namespace: "{{ odf_namespace }}"
          spec:
            manageNodes: false
            monDataDirHostPath: /var/lib/rook
            storageDeviceSets:
              - name: ocs-deviceset
                count: "{{ replica_count }}"
                dataPVCTemplate:
                  spec:
                    accessModes:
                      - ReadWriteOnce
                    resources:
                      requests:
                        storage: "{{ storage_capacity }}"
                    storageClassName: gp3-csi
                    volumeMode: Block
                placement: {}
                portable: true
                replica: 1
                resources: {}

    - name: Wait for StorageCluster to report Ready phase
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: ocs.openshift.io/v1
        kind: StorageCluster
        name: "{{ storage_cluster_name }}"
        namespace: "{{ odf_namespace }}"
      register: storage_cluster_check
      until: >
        storage_cluster_check.resources | length > 0 and
        (storage_cluster_check.resources[0].status.phase | default('')) == 'Ready'
      retries: 40
      delay: 30
EOF
Deliverable: playbooks/deploy-odf.yml is syntactically valid and complete. Validate the YAML and Ansible syntax without touching a live cluster:

ansible-playbook playbooks/deploy-odf.yml --syntax-check
Expected result: playbook: playbooks/deploy-odf.yml is printed with no errors.

If you have access to a real cluster and are authorized to install operators, run it with:

ansible-playbook playbooks/deploy-odf.yml -v
Expect the play to complete with failed=0. The StorageCluster wait step may legitimately take 10-20 minutes on real hardware; in a constrained lab environment you may stop after the subscription is created and proceed to the next tasks, since Task 2 and Task 3 do not require a fully reconciled StorageCluster.

Subtask 1.4: Validate the Deployment
cat > playbooks/validate-odf.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Validate ODF Deployment
  hosts: localhost
  gather_facts: false
  vars_files:
    - ../vars/odf-config.yml

  tasks:
    - name: List deployments in the ODF namespace
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: apps/v1
        kind: Deployment
        namespace: "{{ odf_namespace }}"
      register: odf_deployments

    - name: Display deployment availability
      debug:
        msg: "Deployment {{ item.metadata.name }} available replicas: {{ item.status.availableReplicas | default(0) }}"
      loop: "{{ odf_deployments.resources }}"

    - name: Check StorageCluster status
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: ocs.openshift.io/v1
        kind: StorageCluster
        name: "{{ storage_cluster_name }}"
        namespace: "{{ odf_namespace }}"
      register: storage_cluster_status

    - name: Display StorageCluster phase
      debug:
        msg: "StorageCluster phase: {{ storage_cluster_status.resources[0].status.phase }}"
      when: storage_cluster_status.resources | length > 0

    - name: Check available StorageClasses
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: storage.k8s.io/v1
        kind: StorageClass
      register: storage_classes

    - name: Display ODF-related StorageClasses
      debug:
        msg: "Found StorageClass: {{ item.metadata.name }}"
      loop: "{{ storage_classes.resources }}"
      when: "'ocs' in item.metadata.name"
EOF
ansible-playbook playbooks/validate-odf.yml --syntax-check
Deliverable: playbooks/validate-odf.yml passes --syntax-check. If run against a live cluster with ansible-playbook playbooks/validate-odf.yml -v, expect debug output listing each deployment's available replica count, the StorageCluster phase, and any StorageClass whose name contains ocs.

Task 2: Automate PVC and OBC Creation
Subtask 2.1: Create a PVC Template and PVC Automation Playbook
Ansible's kubernetes.core.k8s module accepts resource definitions either as inline YAML (under definition:) or rendered from a Jinja2 template file using the template module combined with k8s. Both are valid patterns in production. In this subtask you will build the reusable template first, then see it actually consumed by the playbook using the template lookup, so the connection between the two is explicit.

Create the PVC Template
mkdir -p templates/storage
cat > templates/storage/pvc-template.yml.j2 << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: "{{ item.name }}"
  namespace: "{{ item.namespace }}"
  labels:
    app: "{{ item.app_label }}"
    created-by: ansible
spec:
  accessModes:
    - "{{ item.access_mode }}"
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: "{{ item.size }}"
  storageClassName: "{{ item.storage_class }}"
EOF
This template demonstrates Jinja2 templating for Kubernetes manifests: it is rendered per loop item using the template lookup and passed to kubernetes.core.k8s via the definition parameter, so the same file drives every PVC in the list below.

Create the PVC Automation Playbook
cat > playbooks/create-pvcs.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Automate PVC Creation
  hosts: localhost
  gather_facts: false
  vars:
    pvcs_to_create:
      - name: app-data-pvc
        namespace: default
        size: 10Gi
        storage_class: ocs-storagecluster-ceph-rbd
        access_mode: ReadWriteOnce
        app_label: web-app
      - name: database-pvc
        namespace: default
        size: 50Gi
        storage_class: ocs-storagecluster-ceph-rbd
        access_mode: ReadWriteOnce
        app_label: database
      - name: shared-storage-pvc
        namespace: default
        size: 100Gi
        storage_class: ocs-storagecluster-cephfs
        access_mode: ReadWriteMany
        app_label: shared-app

  tasks:
    - name: Create namespaces if they do not exist
      kubernetes.core.k8s:
        name: "{{ item.namespace }}"
        api_version: v1
        kind: Namespace
        state: present
      loop: "{{ pvcs_to_create }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.namespace }}"

    - name: Create PVCs by rendering the pvc-template
      kubernetes.core.k8s:
        state: present
        definition: "{{ lookup('template', '../templates/storage/pvc-template.yml.j2') }}"
      loop: "{{ pvcs_to_create }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.name }}"

    - name: Wait for PVCs to be bound
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: v1
        kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
        name: "{{ item.name }}"
        namespace: "{{ item.namespace }}"
      register: pvc_status
      until: >
        pvc_status.resources | length > 0 and
        pvc_status.resources[0].status.phase == 'Bound'
      retries: 15
      delay: 10
      loop: "{{ pvcs_to_create }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.name }}"

    - name: Show PVC status
      debug:
        msg: "PVC {{ item.item.name }} phase: {{ item.resources[0].status.phase | default('Unknown') }}"
      loop: "{{ pvc_status.results }}"
      when: item.resources | length > 0
EOF
Deliverable: templates/storage/pvc-template.yml.j2 exists and is referenced by playbooks/create-pvcs.yml through lookup('template', ...). Validate:

ansible-playbook playbooks/create-pvcs.yml --syntax-check
Expected result: syntax check passes. If run against a live cluster (ansible-playbook playbooks/create-pvcs.yml -v), expect three PVCs created in the default namespace, and debug output showing each one's phase as Bound (assuming the referenced StorageClasses exist and can satisfy the request).

Subtask 2.2: Create OBC Automation Playbook
Object Bucket Claims (OBCs) provision S3-compatible buckets backed by the ODF RGW (Ceph Object Gateway). This playbook follows the same inline-definition pattern used in Task 1 for clarity, since OBC specs are short and do not benefit as much from a separate template.

cat > playbooks/create-obcs.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Automate OBC Creation
  hosts: localhost
  gather_facts: false
  vars:
    obcs_to_create:
      - name: app-backup-obc
        namespace: default
        storage_class: ocs-storagecluster-ceph-rgw
        app_label: backup-service
      - name: media-storage-obc
        namespace: default
        storage_class: ocs-storagecluster-ceph-rgw
        app_label: media-app
      - name: log-archive-obc
        namespace: logging
        storage_class: ocs-storagecluster-ceph-rgw
        app_label: log-archiver

  tasks:
    - name: Create namespaces if they do not exist
      kubernetes.core.k8s:
        name: "{{ item.namespace }}"
        api_version: v1
        kind: Namespace
        state: present
      loop: "{{ obcs_to_create }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.namespace }}"

    - name: Create OBCs
      kubernetes.core.k8s:
        state: present
        definition:
          apiVersion: objectbucket.io/v1alpha1
          kind: ObjectBucketClaim
          metadata:
            name: "{{ item.name }}"
            namespace: "{{ item.namespace }}"
            labels:
              app: "{{ item.app_label }}"
              created-by: ansible
          spec:
            generateBucketName: "{{ item.name }}"
            storageClassName: "{{ item.storage_class }}"
      loop: "{{ obcs_to_create }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.name }}"

    - name: Wait for OBCs to be bound
      kubernetes.core.k8s_info:
        api_version: objectbucket.io/v1alpha1
        kind: ObjectBucketClaim
        name: "{{ item.name }}"
        namespace: "{{ item.namespace }}"
      register: obc_status
      until: >
        obc_status.resources | length > 0 and
        obc_status.resources[0].status.phase == 'Bound'
      retries: 15
      delay: 10
      loop: "{{ obcs_to_create }}"
      loop_control:
        label: "{{ item.name }}"

    - name: Display OBC bound status
      debug:
        msg: "OBC {{ item.item.name }} phase: {{ item.resources[0].status.phase | default('Unknown') }} in namespace {{ item.item.namespace }}"
      loop: "{{ obc_status.results }}"
      when: item.resources | length > 0
EOF
Deliverable: playbooks/create-obcs.yml passes --syntax-check:

ansible-playbook playbooks/create-obcs.yml --syntax-check
If run against a live cluster, expect three OBCs created (two in default, one in logging), each reaching Bound phase, with a generated Secret and ConfigMap of the same name as each OBC created automatically by the ODF bucket provisioner. Verify with:

oc get obc --all-namespaces
oc get secret app-backup-obc -n default -o jsonpath='{.data.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID}'
