Troubleshooting
Symptom: kubernetes.core.k8s tasks fail with Couldn't find 'name' in the definition or similar module errors. Diagnostic hint: Check indentation and confirm every definition: block has apiVersion, kind, and metadata.name set; YAML indentation errors are the most common cause of this failure.

Symptom: k8s_info wait loops for PVCs or OBCs never reach Bound and eventually exceed retries. Diagnostic hint: Run oc describe pvc <name> -n <namespace> or oc describe obc <name> -n <namespace> to see provisioning events; this usually indicates the referenced StorageClass does not exist yet or the ODF StorageCluster has not finished reconciling.

Conclusion
In this lab you built a working Ansible automation project for OpenShift Data Foundation, covering operator-based ODF deployment, templated PVC provisioning, inline OBC creation, and a health/capacity reporting playbook that reads real StorageCluster and CephCluster status fields. Along the way you practiced two complementary Ansible patterns for generating Kubernetes manifests, the template lookup for reusable, parameterized definitions and inline definition blocks for simpler one-off resources, and you learned to validate playbooks with --syntax-check before ever touching a live cluster. These playbooks are designed to be idempotent, so re-running them against an already-configured cluster is safe and is the basis for wiring them into a CI/CD pipeline (for example, a pipeline stage that runs ansible-playbook playbooks/validate-odf.yml after every cluster change, or a scheduled job that runs manage-odf.yml and forwards its debug output to a monitoring system). For further learning, review the kubernetes.core collection documentation, the Red Hat OpenShift Data Foundation documentation for the StorageCluster and CephCluster custom resource specs, and Ansible's own documentation on idempotency and error handling in production automation.
